# GoVecDB — insert path, end to end (DB.Add / DB.AddBatch)
#
# The ordering constraint is the whole point: the WAL append is the COMMIT
# POINT. Apply-to-graph happens after, and is safe to redo on replay. Reverse
# these two and a crash between them means you acked a write that no longer
# exists.
#
# This is the root package's DB. Collections (service.Manager) sit a layer
# above it and add nothing to this path but a borrow, so they are not drawn.

vars: {
  d2-config: {
    theme-id: 200
    layout-engine: elk
    pad: 40
  }
}


# Black canvas. Theme 200 makes default label/markdown text light;
# every explicit fill below is a dark surface chosen to keep its hue.
style.fill: "#000000"

direction: down

classes: {
  step: {
    style.fill: "#0D2137"
    style.stroke: "#4FC3F7"
    style.stroke-width: 2
  }
  critical: {
    style.fill: "#3B1215"
    style.stroke: "#FF6B6B"
    style.stroke-width: 3
  }
  wal: {
    style.fill: "#33240E"
    style.stroke: "#FFB74D"
    style.stroke-width: 2
  }
  graph: {
    style.fill: "#191B33"
    style.stroke: "#9FA8DA"
  }
  disk: {
    shape: cylinder
    style.fill: "#0E2A16"
    style.stroke: "#66BB6A"
  }
  note: {
    shape: text
    style.font-size: 13
    style.italic: true
  }
}

start: "Add(Vector) · AddBatch([]Vector)" {
  shape: circle
  style.fill: "#3A2B52"
  style.stroke: "#B39DDB"
}

s1: 1 · Validate — no lock held {
  class: step
  body: |md
    id: non-empty, ≤ 512 B, valid UTF-8
    values: exactly the dimension fixed at `Open`
    — every one finite (NaN / ±Inf refused)
    metadata: ≤ 256 keys, string · bool · int64 · float64
    AddBatch: ≤ 10,000, **all** checked before any is logged
    (defaults; `WithLimits` raises them up to hard ceilings)
  |
}

s2: "2 · Queue, take writeMu, write the group" {
  class: step
  body: |md
    Queue a `putRequest`, then take writeMu. Whoever holds it
    writes **every** queued request as one group (FIFO, ≤ maxBatch
    vectors, never splitting one) — a caller whose request was
    written meanwhile just returns its result. One group at a time
    across append **and** apply, so apply order is log order.
    Closed, `WithReadOnly`, or a past durability failure → refused.
  |
}

# Sequence numbers used to be a separate DB step. They are assigned inside
# the WAL writer, under its own lock, because only the lock holder knows the
# order records were written in.
wal_block: 3 · WAL APPEND — the commit point (under writeMu) {
  class: critical

  encode: encodePut + record frame {
    class: wal
    fmt: |md
      `[crc32c][type=PUT][seq][len]` — 17 B header
      payload = `[version][idLen][id][dim][float32s][meta]`
      crc covers type, seq **and** len, not just payload
      seq assigned by the writer — strictly increasing
    |
  }

  append: Write into 64 KiB bufio buffer {
    class: wal
    body: |md
      User space, not the kernel. Rotates at
      `MaxSegmentBytes`, fsyncing the old segment first.
      A group streams its n records through the same buffer.
    |
  }

  fsync: SyncPolicy gate {
    class: wal
    body: |md
      **SyncAlways** (zero value) — fsync before return, ~4.24 ms
      A group — one batch, or many writers' Adds — pays **one** fsync
      **SyncInterval** — ticker fsyncs every 50 ms (default)
      **SyncNever** — ~780 ns; flushed when the buffer fills or on Close
    |
  }

  fail: "Any write/fsync error → db.fail" {
    style.fill: "#3B1215"
    style.stroke: "#FF6B6B"
    body: |md
      Sticky: the DB is permanently `ErrReadOnly`,
      reads keep working. Emits `DurabilityFailure`
      (under writeMu — the observer must not call back).
    |
  }

  encode -> append -> fsync
  fsync -> fail: "error" {
    style.stroke-dash: 3
    style.stroke: "#FF6B6B"
  }
}

durable_note: |md
  **Under SyncAlways, "returned nil" means durable** — for every
  record of a batch. Under SyncInterval / SyncNever an acknowledged
  write may still sit in the user-space buffer, so a *process* crash
  loses it too, not just power loss. A documented choice
  (see docs/DURABILITY.md), never the default.
| {
  class: note
}

s4: 4 · Apply — under applyMu (exclusive), still under writeMu {
  class: critical

  route: "one vector → applyPut · a group → applyPuts" {
    class: step
    body: |md
      **One** hold of applyMu covers `index.Insert` **and**
      `store.Put`, so no reader sees (v2, md1).
      A batch keeps the last occurrence of each id and applies
      in groups of `applyGroup = max(64, 4 × workers)`,
      releasing applyMu between groups.
    |
  }

  copy: "prepare(v) — copy + normalize, before g.mu" {
    class: graph
    body: |md
      Copies so the graph never aliases a caller buffer.
      Normalizes for Cosine. Done outside the graph's
      lock: it touches only the caller's slice and config.
    |
  }

  upsert: "4a · Is this id already live? — the upsert branch (g.mu held)" {
    class: graph
    style.stroke: "#FFD54F"
    style.stroke-width: 2

    same: "ids[id] hit, same vector → return nil" {
      style.fill: "#332B0D"
      style.stroke: "#FFD54F"
      body: |md
        `slices.Equal` against the **stored** form.
        No tombstone, no slot, no edge touched.

        This is the WAL-replay path: recovery re-applies
        records the graph already holds across a snapshot
        boundary, and if that cost a slot every restart
        would inflate the graph with tombstones.
        **~331 ns**, against 776 µs for a real replacement.
      |
    }

    kill: "ids[id] hit, new vector → tombstone the old slot" {
      style.fill: "#3B1215"
      style.stroke: "#FF6B6B"
      style.stroke-width: 2
      body: |md
        1. `delete(ids, id)` — the **binding** goes
        2. `node.deleted = true` — the **slot** stays,
           keeping its index and every edge it has
        3. `numDeleted++`
        4. was this slot the entry? → `reelectEntry()`,
           or the descent below starts from a corpse

        Inside `Insert`'s own write lock, never by calling
        `Delete` — that would open a window where the id
        belongs to nobody, and a reader would observe an
        update as a **disappearance**.
      |
    }

    same -> kill: "vector differs" {
      style.stroke: "#FFD54F"
    }
  }

  why_new_slot: |md
    **Why not just overwrite `node.vector` in place?**

    The slot's index is referenced by neighbor lists all over
    the graph, and those edges were chosen for the OLD vector —
    they claim "these two are close" about a pair that no longer
    is. Searches would keep being routed in from a region the
    vector has left.

    Repairing them is not available either: pruning makes edges
    asymmetric, so a node's own neighbor list is *not* the list
    of nodes pointing at it. Finding every inbound edge means
    scanning the whole graph, **O(N·M) per update**.

    A fresh slot is correct by construction. It costs one dead
    slot — the same debt `Delete` takes on, paid to `Compact`.
  | {
    class: note
  }

  lvl: "randomLevel() + place() — a brand-new slot" {
    class: graph
    body: |md
      `int(-log(rand) * ml)`, `ml = 1/ln(M)`
      Seeded RNG; a **serial** build is reproducible,
      a parallel batch is not (`WithInsertWorkers(1)`).
      A replacement draws a **fresh** level.
    |
  }

  descend: Greedy descend maxLevel → level+1 {
    class: graph
    body: ef=1, just to find a good entry point
  }

  connect: "link() — for each layer min(level, maxLevel) → 0" {
    class: graph
    body: |md
      1. `searchLayer(vec, cur, EfConstruction, lc, nil)`
         — a build never filters
      2. `selectNeighbors(w, maxConn(lc))` — alpha 1.0
      3. `connect(new, nb)` **and** `connect(nb, new)`
      4. `pruneConnections` — trim to cap, demoting tombstones
      5. seed next layer with the closest hit
    |
  }

  promote: Promote to entry point {
    class: graph
    body: if level > maxLevel
  }

  batch: "InsertBatch — per group, in chunks of 4 × workers" {
    class: graph
    style.stroke: "#4FC3F7"
    body: |md
      Each chunk holds `g.mu` throughout; readers get it back between.
      Workers = `min(workers, live / 32)` — a small graph grows serially.
      1. serial: upsert rule, `randomLevel` in batch order, `place`
      2. a node above `maxLevel` is linked **alone** → entry fixed
      3. parallel `link()`: striped `nodeLocks` (4,096 · 32 KiB),
         never two held; `node.linking` hides in-flight nodes
         from other workers' traversals
    |
  }

  meta: "store.Put(id, metadata)" {
    class: step
    body: |md
      In-RAM `store.Map`, keys and strings interned.
      Durable only through the WAL record and the
      snapshot's second section — no payload file.
    |
  }

  route -> copy: "Add / small batch"
  route -> batch: "batch ≥ 2" {
    style.stroke: "#4FC3F7"
  }
  batch -> upsert: "same rule, per node" {
    style.stroke-dash: 3
  }
  copy -> upsert.same: "id already live"
  copy -> lvl: "new id — branch skipped" {
    style.stroke-dash: 3
  }
  upsert.kill -> lvl: "append a new slot at len(nodes)"
  lvl -> descend -> connect -> promote
  promote -> meta
  batch -> meta: "group linked" {
    style.stroke: "#4FC3F7"
  }

  upsert -> why_new_slot: {
    style.stroke-dash: 4
    style.opacity: 0.7
    style.stroke: "#6E6E6E"
  }
}

readers_note: |md
  **Readers never take writeMu**, so they never wait on the fsync.
  A search waits at most for one apply — one group of a batch —
  which is what the graph's own write lock already costs it.
| {
  class: note
}

s5: 5 · kickCalibration(Len) {
  class: step
  body: |md
    Non-blocking send on a 1-slot channel, only when
    the live count has doubled or halved. The
    calibrator runs in its own goroutine.
  |
}

s6: "6 · Ack — mark every request in the group done, release writeMu" {
  shape: circle
  style.fill: "#1B4D2E"
  style.stroke: "#66BB6A"
}

wal_disk: "WAL segments on disk (wal-NNNNNN.log)" {
  class: disk
  style.multiple: true
}

crash: Crash here? {
  shape: diamond
  style.fill: "#4A1418"
  style.stroke: "#EF5350"
}

crash_note: |md
  **Before the append returns** → no ack was given. A torn
  tail fails its checksum; replay truncates that segment
  *logically* at the tear and moves on. The write never happened.

  **After the append, before step 6** → the record is in the log
  (under SyncAlways, on disk). Replay re-applies it. A client retry
  is harmless: PUT is an upsert, and an unchanged vector costs nothing.

  **Index error after a durable append** → the call fails, but the
  record replays on the next start. Log and index disagree until
  then — the direction the ordering rule chooses on purpose.

  **AddBatch failing midway** → a durable prefix may exist; the DB is
  read-only, and the next start replays that prefix.
| {
  class: note
}

start -> s1 -> s2 -> wal_block.encode

wal_block.fsync -> wal_disk: durable {
  style.stroke: "#FFB74D"
  style.stroke-width: 3
}

wal_block.fsync -> s4.route: "ONLY NOW apply" {
  style.stroke: "#FF6B6B"
  style.stroke-width: 3
  style.bold: true
}

s4.meta -> s5 -> s6

s4.upsert.same -> s4.meta: "unchanged → graph untouched, metadata still written" {
  style.stroke: "#FFD54F"
  style.stroke-dash: 3
}

wal_block -> crash: {
  style.stroke-dash: 3
  style.opacity: 0.75
}
crash -> crash_note: {
  style.stroke-dash: 3
  style.opacity: 0.75
}

# --- annotations, drawn faint so they never compete with the flow ---
wal_block -> durable_note: {
  style.stroke-dash: 4
  style.opacity: 0.7
  style.stroke: "#6E6E6E"
}
s4 -> readers_note: {
  style.stroke-dash: 4
  style.opacity: 0.7
  style.stroke: "#6E6E6E"
}
